hed thank you um so  let me rephrase it a little bit our our  vaccine task force i mean and yes with  the additional measures but should they  either be scrapped or should they be  upgraded to three doses  what would the best  science be on this  so i i think we have to be realistic um  we have 6.6 million ontarians that have  come forward for their third doses we're  not going to hold back uh and mandate uh  through proof of vaccination that uh  five more million ontarians get  immunized with third doses two doses is  exceptionally protective against  admission to hospital  it does protect you against uh  ending up in an intensive care unit and  or death it's done its job protecting  ontarians and because 12 plus we have  over 90 percent protection now  i think it's an a prudent time to review  uh proof of vaccination as one tool in a  multi-layered approach uh for for these  venues  next  question from colin demelo at ctv news  hey good afternoon dr moore so some  people in ontario might be looking at  provinces like alberta and saskatchewan  and wondering uh what is taking ontario  so long so could you outline what is the  difference that you see in alberta for  instance that allows that province to  remove all of its covet 19 restrictions  almost immediately while we take a  slower approach what's the difference  uh to be honest colin i don't spend a  lot of time focusing on alberta or other  provinces i am focused on ontario and i  think our plan is reasonable so if you  recall we've built the date for february  21st of reopening  that date  may be reviewed next week given the  significant improvements in all of the  key metrics in ontario  as well you may recall we had a date of  march 14th for subsequent opening of  mass gathering events i think given the  rapid decline in all our case counts we  will follow ontario data we will make  ontario based plans we will continue to  follow our gradual phased approach it  has worked so well for us  and protected so many ontarians and just  recently has been able to i think  mitigate the the number of ontarians  having to end up in hospital and  intensive care unit settings i thank all  ontarians for their sacrifices uh  because it has helped us all  collectively protect our health system  and we're in a very good position to  reconsider timelines  as well as all public health measures  eventually these are government  decisions  but we will make an ontario focused  ontario-based data-driven science-driven  decision by government in the in the  coming week  okay thank you so it sounds like  those dates that we have the 21st and  the march 14th are  almost no longer going to be in effect  that you could potentially move them up  by a few days if not a week  i'm wondering so what can businesses  expect here capacity is that going to be  moved up or what measures are you  willing to speed up and which will be  the last to return so a large sporting  event would that be the last to return  capacity limits could that be sped up  give us give us your your take on dates  and what could change i i would look at  um the dates that have been provided feb  february 21st uh and march 14th all  those measures that would be opened it  it's now given  how well ontario's done in the omicron  wave how well  our health system is recovering uh i i  would assume government wants and will  review timelines as well as all public  health measures including when we remove  proof of vaccination when we remove  masking in certain venues i do think  masking in public indoor spaces like  transit subways buses  will be maintained  until we really have very low endemicity  and that would be cases below 10 per 100  000  on a weekly average  which would have correlates with  hospitalization uh and icu  utilization as well  follow-up  i already have my two you can go to this  thank you last question  from holly mackenzie suiter canadian  press  hi dr moore i just wanted to get a  little more clarity on you said 